.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBpstring_t\fR structure describes strings using 4 variables:
.IP \(bu 2
string's character buffer (\fBpstrbuf\fR),
.IP \(bu 2
the number of characters inside the buffer (\fBpstrlen\fR),
.IP \(bu 2
buffer's size/capacity (\fBpstrcap\fR),
.IP \(bu 2
allocator which owns the buffer's memory (\fBpstrallocator\fR).
.PP
Strings initialized with \fB{0}\fR or created using \fBpstrnew\fR, \fBpstralloc\fR,
\fBpstrdup\fR and others, own their buffers and can be resized and expanded,
but require calling \fBpstrfree\fR to free memory resources.
.PP
Strings can also be initialized as slices using \fBpstrwrap\fR, \fBpstrslice\fR and
\fBpstrrange\fR. Since slices represent chunks of other strings, they don't
need to be freed, but their buffer needs to be valid during their usage.
.PP
Shorter strings can be stored directly in the \fBpstring_t\fR using SSO,
provided they have been allocated using the default allocator. This mechanism
is not noticable most of the time, but can cause obscure bugs if misused.
.SH REFERENCE
.SS pstring_error
.PP
Error codes are negated versions of POSIX ones.
.SS pstrcatch
.PP
This function set's up the exception handler for the \fB<pf_exception.h>\fR
exception handling interface. The first call will return \fBNULL\fR, after
which exception\-throwing code can be executed.
.PP
The second return value will represent the emitted exception.
.RS 4
.PP
The global exception stack is thread local.
.RE
.SS PSTRTHROW
.PP
These macros emit an exception with the given error code and message.
.SS pstrthrow
.PP
Emits an exception with given message.
.SS pstrthrowf
.PP
Emits an exception with given formatting.
.SS pstrvthrowf
.PP
Emits an exception with given formatting.
.SS pstrrethrow
.PP
Emits the exception \fBe\fR for the next catcher.
.SS pstrdetect
.PP
When \fBPSTRING_DETECT\fR is defined, this function detects the
SIMD capabilities of the CPU at runtime. Otherwise, the function
immediately exits, while the SIMD detection occurs at compile\-time.
.SS pstrbuf
.PP
Returns the character buffer of \fBstr\fR. If \fBstr\fR is resized after calling
this function, the returned pointer should be considered invalid.
.SS pstrlen
.PP
Returns the length, number of bytes, of \fBstr\fR.
.SS pstrcap
.PP
Returns the number of bytes allocated by \fBstr\fR.
.SS pstrallocator
.PP
Returns the allocator used by \fBstr\fR or \fBNULL\fR if it's a slice.
.SS pstrsso
.PP
Checks if \fBstr\fR is stored on the stack.
.SS pstrowned
.PP
Checks if \fBstr\fR can be resized (not a slice).
.SS pstrend
.PP
Returns the address pointing to the end of the character buffer,
which, if \fBstr\fR is owned, points to a \fB\e0\fR character.
.SS pstrget
.PP
Returns the character at index \fBi\fR or \fB'\e0'\fR if out of bounds
.SS pstrslot
.PP
Returns the pointer to the slot \fBi\fR or \fBNULL\fR if out of bounds
.SS pstrnew
.PP
Initializes \fBout\fR by copying the contents of \fBstr\fR.
If \fBlen\fR is \fB0\fR and \fBstr\fR is not empty, \fBstrlen\fR is called.
If \fBalloc\fR is \fBNULL\fR, the standard allocator is used.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM.
.SS pstrdup
.PP
Copies the contents of \fBstr\fR into \fBout\fR, like \fBpstrnew\fR.
You can use this function to turn slices into owned pstrings.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM.
.SS pstralloc
.PP
Initializes \fBout\fR and reserves \fBcapacity\fR bytes.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM.
.SS pstrfree
.PP
Frees all resources used by \fBstr\fR, if it is owned.
.SS pstrarray_free
.PP
Frees all strings in array \fBa\fR, if they are owned.
.SS pstrdump
.PP
Copies the contents of \fBstr\fR to \fBbuffer\fR as a null\-terminated string.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM.
.SS pstrterm
.PP
Ensures that \fBstr\fR is null\-terminated by copying it's content to a new
buffer and appending '\e0', if it's not currently null\-terminated.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM.
.SS pstrterms
.PP
Ensures that \fBstr\fR is null\-terminated by copying it's content to the given
buffer and appending '\e0', if it's not currently null\-terminated.
Returned string is either \fBbuffer\fR or \fBpstrbuf(str)\fR.
.SS pstristerm
.PP
Checks if \fBstr\fR is null\-terminated.
.SS pstrunwrap
.PP
Returns the character buffer of \fBstr\fR. If \fBstr\fR is using SSO,
the contents of \fBstr\fR will be copied to a brand new buffer.
.SS pstrwrap
.PP
Initializes \fBout\fR as a slice, using the \fBbuffer\fR for storage.
If \fBlength\fR is \fB0\fR, \fBstrlen\fR is used to calculate it's length.
If \fBlength\fR is \fB0\fR and capacity is not, \fBstrnlen\fR is used instead.
If \fBcapacity\fR is \fB0\fR it is set to the computed length.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL.
.SS pstrwrapb
.PP
Initializes \fBout\fR as a slice, using the \fBbuffer\fR for storage.
No \fBstrlen\fR is performed by this variant.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL.
.SS pstrslice
.PP
Initializes \fBout\fR as a slice of bytes from \fBstr\fR, starting at \fBfrom\fR
(inclusive) and ending at \fBto\fR (exclusive). Both indices are set to
the length of \fBstr\fR if they are larger. If \fBto\fR is smaller than
\fBfrom\fR, a zero\-length slice at \fBto\fR is taken.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL.
.SS pstrcut
.PP
Removes characters around the specified slice of bytes from \fBstr\fR,
starting at \fBfrom\fR (inclusive) and ending at \fBto\fR (exclusive).
Both indices are set to the length of \fBstr\fR if they are larger.
If \fBto\fR is smaller than \fBfrom\fR, the length of \fBstr\fR is set to zero.
.RS 4
.PP
If \fBstr\fR is a slice, it is repositioned instead.
.RE
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL.
.SS pstrrange
.PP
Initializes \fBout\fR as a range of bytes from \fBstr\fR, starting
at \fBfrom\fR (inclusive) and ending at \fBto\fR (exclusive).
If \fBfrom\fR is \fBNULL\fR or invalid, it's set to the start of the pstring.
If \fBto\fR is \fBNULL\fR or invalid, it's set to the end of the pstring.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL.
.SS PSTRWRAP
.PP
Initializes a \fBpstring_t\fR as a slice of \fBstr\fR, which is assumed
to be a null\-terminated statically allocated string/array.
.SS PSTR
.PP
Returns a reference to \fBpstring_t\fR that is initialized as a slice of \fBstr\fR,
which is assumed to be a null\-terminated statically allocated string/array.
.SS pstrreserve
.PP
Reserves space to fit additional \fBcount\fR items in \fBstr\fR
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM.
.SS pstrgrow
.PP
Extends \fBstr\fR's buffer by at least \fBcount\fR bytes.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM.
.SS pstrshrink
.PP
Shrinks \fBstr\fR's buffer to use as little space as possible.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM.
.SS pstrclear
.PP
Removes all characters from \fBstr\fR, setting it's length to 0.
.SS pstrequal
.PP
Checks if \fBleft\fR and \fBright\fR pstring are equal.
.SS pstrequals
.PP
Checks if \fBleft\fR pstring and \fBright\fR string are equal.
If \fBlength\fR is zero, \fBright\fR is treated as a null\-terminated string.
.SS pstrcmp
.PP
Compares \fBleft\fR and \fBright\fR lexicographically, returning:
.IP \(bu 2
a positive number if \fBleft\fR should appear before \fBright\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
a negative number if \fBleft\fR should appear after \fBright\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB0\fR if they are equal.
.SS pstrcpy
.PP
Copies the contents of \fBsrc\fR into \fBdst\fR
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM.
.SS pstrhash
.PP
Returns a non\-unique integer value representing the contents of \fBstr\fR.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM.
.SS pstrdistance
.PP
Returns Damerau–Levenshtein distance between \fBleft\fR and \fBright\fR.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM.
.SS pstrshift
.PP
This function slices \fBstr\fR by moving the starting index by \fBright\fR and
the ending index by \fBleft\fR number of characters. (\fBstr\fR must be a slice)
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL.
.SS pstrprefix
.PP
Checks if \fBstr\fR starts with a \fBprefix\fR.
.SS pstrsuffix
.PP
Checks if \fBstr\fR ends with a \fBsuffix\fR.
.SS pstrenv
.PP
Gets the value of the environment variable \fBname\fR and slices it as \fBout\fR.
Object \fBname\fR can be a slice, i.e. null termination is not required.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOENT, ENOMEM.
.SS pstr__setlen
.PP
Naively sets the length of \fBstr\fR to \fBlength\fR
